{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Why Moses and Aaron</b> — He asked them their names and mentioned them by name, as a mark of honor." "<b>You distract the people from their labors</b> — Onkelos renders (Onkelos on Exodus 5:4) תבטלו (\"you nullify\"), and likewise פורע מוסר (Proverbs 13:18), and ותפרעו כל עצתי (ibid. 1:25)." "<b>Go to your burdens</b> — By way of the plain sense, this refers to the king's labor, for they were among the people, since on this occasion they came before him with all the people. He did not listen to them and commanded them, \"All of you, return to the labor.\" And Moses and Aaron returned before him (Exodus 7:10), and Pharaoh said to them, \"Give us a sign,\" and they did so. Then in his eyes they were like sorcerers and magicians and sages, and he treated them with honor. But from the time the plagues began upon him, he treated them with great fear. And likewise it appears further that not all the people of Israel labored all the days for Pharaoh in mortar and bricks, for they filled the land of Egypt with cities. Rather they performed labor as a levy (מס); only when he made their yoke heavy upon them did he take many of them." "And Rashi explained (Rashi on Exodus 5:4) <b>לסבלותיכם (to your burdens)</b> — to the work that you have to do in your own houses, but the labor of Egypt's bondage was not upon the tribe of Levi. And you may know this, for Moses and Aaron would go out and come in freely. And this is correct, for all of a person's labor in the house and in the field is called by this term, as in לכל סבל בית יוסף (I Kings 11:28). And it is the custom of every nation to have among them sages who teach their wisdom, and therefore Pharaoh left the tribe of Levi alone, for they were their sages and elders. And all of this was a cause from before HaShem. And in these words of Shemot Rabbah (5:16) I found: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said — the tribe of Levi was free from crushing labor. Pharaoh said to them: because you are free, you say \"let us go and sacrifice to our God\"? Go to your burdens — to Israel. Another interpretation: he said to them — it is enough for you that you stand free; perhaps it is bad for you that you do no labor? Go to your burdens. And Rabbi Abraham explained <b>לכו לסבלותיכם (go to your burdens)</b> — the burdens of the people, for he was speaking with them on behalf of all Israel."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :register {:flags {3 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:52.105942409Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}